# own_native_riscv64.tkr — the riscv64-linux configuration of the own-backend corpus channel.
#
# The SAME program as own_native.tkr, built for riscv64-linux and run under qemu — the corpus the
# retired riscv64-qemu CI lane drove. A second `.tkr` rather than a second `Examples` column because
# the project build cache is keyed on the build SHAPE and not on the env: two targets in one file
# would have the second one assert the first one's artifact.
#
# `own_riscv_object_well_formed` is why this channel is a PROJECT at all. That scenario runs the
# retired lane's ELF cross-check over the emitted `.o`, and the snippet fold refuses any row carrying
# a leaf-tool check (in a folded build the inspected object belongs to the whole fold, not to the row
# that asked). As a project row it inspects THIS file's one build, so the check costs no build of its
# own — measured: the riscv64 configuration went from two builds to one.
Feature: F2 language corpus — folded EXPECT_EXIT single-file fixtures (dobrado, dir removido)

  Scenario: own_arith_exit (own-native — `n` is a `mut` local, so the MUL/ADD are fold-opaque)
    Given target = "riscv64-linux"
    When built and run
    Then exit = 42

  Scenario: own_sub_exit (own-native — same fold-opacity rationale, MUL/SUB)
    Given target = "riscv64-linux"
    When built and run
    Then exit = 42


Feature: F5 own-native corpus — os cenarios que o backend nativo tem de fechar sozinho (target Examples: host E cross para riscv64 sob qemu; herda o CORPUS do extinto scripts/diff_c_own.sh e a lane riscv64-qemu que ele dirigia). O EIXO backend morreu com o emissor de C: cada cenario afirma o proprio exit/stdout/trap, nao a igualdade com um segundo backend.

